WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.516
How does a strand of DNA become the sticky glue

2
00:00:02.616 --> 00:00:05.411
that holds sand together? It happens in three steps.

3
00:00:06.178 --> 00:00:10.338
First, the gene is copied into a short-lived message called messenger R-N-A.

4
00:00:10.438 --> 00:00:13.758
This copy does not last. it is made and destroyed constantly.

5
00:00:13.845 --> 00:00:15.978
That message is then read to build the enzyme,

6
00:00:16.078 --> 00:00:18.212
the molecular machine that does the real work.

7
00:00:19.611 --> 00:00:22.709
The enzyme grabs glutamate units, one after another,

8
00:00:22.809 --> 00:00:25.722
and stitches them into a long chain, gamma-P-G-A.

9
00:00:25.822 --> 00:00:28.367
This is the polymer that becomes our crust.

10
00:00:29.634 --> 00:00:32.450
But the cell also carries genes whose enzymes chew that

11
00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:34.889
chain back up. So we knock those scissors out.

12
00:00:35.767 --> 00:00:39.435
With no scissors, the gamma-P-G-A no longer gets cut back, so

13
00:00:39.535 --> 00:00:42.276
it piles up, and the crust gets what it needs.

14
00:00:44.167 --> 00:00:48.900
Gene, message, enzyme, polymer, four steps, tracked in time. That is how a

15
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.295
line of code in the DNA turns into glue in the sand.

